<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Nice Weather Today!</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's Raining.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Draw a single picture that represents a simple story or event. Communication: Use gestures to show how to play a very simple game.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can clarify or confirm understanding of weather by pointing to pictures or using simple words. Can draw simple pictures representing the weather and label them with help. Can use gestures or body language to describe weather (e.g., pretending to shiver when it's cold).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students, "Good morning, everyone!" and claps hands for "Let's sing the weather song!" The teacher leads a simple "What's the weather?" song with actions: pointing to a sunny picture and making a sun shape, pretending to shiver for "cold", and making rain gestures for "raining". This reactivates prior knowledge of weather vocabulary and gestures. The teacher asks, "What is the weather today?" and points outside the window, encouraging students to answer with gestures and simple words like "Sunny!" or "Raining!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of "It is sunny." and says the phrase clearly, emphasizing the /s/ sound. Then, the teacher holds up a flashcard of "It is raining." and says the phrase, emphasizing the /r/ sound. The teacher models drawing a simple sun on the board and saying, "It is sunny." Then, the teacher draws simple rain and says, "It is raining." The teacher shows how to draw simple weather icons to represent the weather.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this a cat or the sun?" (Teacher points to a sun picture.) -> Expected answer: "Sun!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I do this (teacher makes rain gesture), is it cold or raining?" -> Expected answer: "Raining!"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Nice Weather Today!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large chart paper or projector displaying a "Weather Journal" template with spaces for drawing the weather and writing a simple sentence. Picture flashcards for "sunny," "raining," "cloudy," "windy," "cold," "warm."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Pragmatic: Talking about weather: Describe simple weather conditions (e.g., "It is sunny.") and describe simple reactions to weather (e.g., "I'm cold." or "I'm hot."). Mediation: Can draw simple pictures representing the weather and label them with help.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activities & Teacher Actions:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher introduces the "Weather Journal" project. "Today, we will learn about our Weather Journal!" The teacher shows an example of a simple journal entry: a drawing of a sun and the sentence "It is sunny."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a picture of "It is raining." and asks, "What can we draw for this in our journal?" (Students might respond: "Rain!"). The teacher draws simple rain in the journal template. "And what sentence can we write?" (Students might say: "It is raining."). The teacher writes "It is raining."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher models how to express personal feelings about weather, for example, drawing a child shivering and saying, "It is cold. I'm cold." The teacher encourages students to mimic the gesture for "cold." (Sociolinguistic: Engaging in simple weather-related conversations.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher displays 3-4 simple weather pictures (sunny, rainy, cloudy) and says a weather condition, e.g., "It is windy." Students point to the corresponding picture. (Mediation: spoken word to picture). The teacher then shows a simple drawing (e.g., a cloud) and asks, "What is this weather?" (Mediation: picture to spoken word/clarification).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Grammar: Present continuous ("It is raining."), Subject + verb + adjective ("It is sunny."). Vocabulary: weather, sun, rain, clouds, wind, sunny, rainy, cloudy, warm, cold, cool, clear, windy. Pronunciation: /s/ in "sunny" and /r/ in "raining". Phonemic awareness: Initial sound of letter /w/ in "weather" and /r/ in "rainy."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1: Weather Match-Up (Controlled Practice)</b><br>
                        The teacher distributes worksheets with simple drawings of different weather conditions (sun, rain clouds, wind) on one side and weather words ("sunny", "raining", "cloudy", "windy") on the other. Students draw a line to match each drawing to its correct word. The teacher monitors and helps with pronunciation, focusing on /s/ and /r/. (Mediation: Text to Picture and vice versa).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2: "Draw the Weather!" Game (Semi-controlled)</b><br>
                        The teacher calls out a weather condition (e.g., "It is windy!"). Students quickly draw a simple picture of windy weather on mini-whiteboards or scratch paper. The teacher encourages students to make a "whoosh" sound for wind. The teacher then says, "What is the weather?" and students hold up their drawings while saying "It is windy!" The teacher prompts for different weather conditions: "It is sunny!", "It is cold! (pretend to shiver)", "It is raining! (make rain gesture)." (Mediation: Spoken word to Drawing/Gesture).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3: Partner Weather Show-and-Tell (Pair Interaction)</b><br>
                        Students work in pairs. Each student draws one simple weather picture on their paper (e.g., a cloud). They then show their drawing to their partner. The partner has to say, "It is [cloudy]!" and make a corresponding gesture if possible. They switch roles. The teacher circulates, offering support and ensuring accurate pronunciation and understanding. (Mediation: Picture to Spoken Word and Gesture).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Nice Weather Today!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Task: My Weather Journal Entry and Explanation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will create their first individual "Weather Journal" entry and then mediate its meaning by explaining it to a peer or the class, using simple drawings, words, and gestures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Guided individual drawing and simple writing, followed by a peer explanation/presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activities & Teacher Actions:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives each student a blank "Weather Journal" page (a piece of paper with a box for drawing and a line for writing).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reminds students of the scenario: "Look outside! What is the weather today? Is it 'Nice Weather Today!' or 'It's Raining.'?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students draw the current day's weather in the drawing box (e.g., sun and clouds if it's partly cloudy). The teacher circulates, providing individual help and asking, "What is the weather?" to prompt vocabulary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">With the teacher's help (e.g., writing the first letter or pointing to word cards), students write a simple sentence below their drawing, such as "It is sunny." or "It is raining." The teacher reminds them to try to identify the initial sound of /w/ in 'weather' and /r/ in 'rainy' if relevant.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In small groups or pairs, students then take turns showing their journal entry. They point to their drawing and say, "It is [sunny/raining/cloudy]." They can also use a gesture to describe their drawing (e.g., pretend to shiver if they drew cold weather). This involves clarifying and confirming understanding of their drawing to their peer.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher encourages peers to ask "What is the weather?" or confirm, "Is it sunny?" to practice asking and answering basic WH-questions.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "My Weather Journal Entry and Explanation" activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student presents their Weather Journal entry. They will point to their drawing of the day's weather and state, "It is [weather condition]." They will also use a gesture or body language to describe a weather condition (e.g., pretending to shiver when it's cold, making a 'windy' motion). This assesses their ability to mediate information through drawing, simple text, and gestures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Drawing:</b> Student draws a recognizable weather condition (e.g., sun, rain, clouds). (Specific Objective: Draw a single picture that represents a simple story or event).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Student uses target weather words (e.g., "sunny", "raining", "cloudy") with help if needed. (Learning Outcome: Can clarify or confirm understanding of weather by pointing to pictures or using simple words).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Intelligible pronunciation of /s/ in "sunny" and /r/ in "raining".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Gestures/Body Language:</b> Student uses appropriate gestures to describe weather (e.g., shivering for cold, hand motion for wind). (Specific Objective: Use gestures to show how to play a very simple game; Learning Outcome: Can use gestures or body language to describe weather).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Simple Sentence Structure:</b> Attempts to use "It is..." for description.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher shows a picture of a sun.) "Can I draw this sun?" (Students nod Yes or No).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I say 'It is sunny'?" (Students give a thumbs up or thumbs down).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7-8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite weather to draw in your journal today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you show me with your hands how you feel when it's cold?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students clarify weather information through drawing and gestures? (e.g., The "Draw the Weather!" game effectively engaged all students and provided immediate visual mediation practice).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for next time? (e.g., Provide more pre-printed sentence starters for the Weather Journal entries for students still struggling with writing. Incorporate more counting practice with numbers 1-20 in a weather context, such as "How many clouds do you see?").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "It's Raining" theme. The next lesson will introduce a new theme and set of vocabulary, potentially building on the idea of describing daily events or feelings, while maintaining the focus on simple communicative tasks for 1st Grade students.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>